1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:01,680
Grokbot has been taking off.

2
00:00:01,806 --> 00:00:06,820
Their paying users who use the app daily increased sixfold in just the past five or so weeks.

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:10,480
People are seriously already starting to integrate their business operations with it.

4
00:00:10,574 --> 00:00:14,852
And I also made an update video on it recently, which a lot of you seem to have found value in.

5
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:23,108
So today I'll go through all of the best Grokbot tips and techniques, all the way from easy wins to some advanced setups, so that if you're not using Grokbot, you can decide if it's for you.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:32,000
Or if you already are, then these techniques can let you do more with your agents without burning through your usage, and even some tips on how to best use it alongside a tool like Cloud Code if you're more advanced.

7
00:00:32,238 --> 00:00:33,440
And if you're new, my name's Jay.

8
00:00:33,614 --> 00:00:42,404
I spent over a decade working with brands you probably know, have been in AI since my master's in data science, and now I'm leading our AI business in one of the largest AI communities globally.

9
00:00:43,054 --> 00:00:44,880
Number one is importing your memory.

10
00:00:45,134 --> 00:00:49,060
Importing your memory or your context is one of the best ways to get started with Grokbot.

11
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:54,960
This is because you probably already talked to a chatbot like ChatGPT or cloud that knows a fair bit about you already.

12
00:00:55,278 --> 00:01:02,880
So if you import your memory from your main AI tool that you've been using into GrokBot, then it will instantly have context on your business and how to best help you from day one.

13
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:04,240
Now there's two ways to do this.

14
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:09,920
The official one with Grokbot right now is if you open settings, then general, you can choose start import there.

15
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:17,680
You can then copy the recommended prompt that they have there into cloud or ChatGPT and paste that other chatbot's answer into the second box right here.

16
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:28,720
Or alternatively, if you haven't been using another AI tool, you can just have your Grokbot interview instead with a simple prompt like this one where it asks you one question at a time in order to build up context around you and your work.

17
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:39,200
Also, if you've been using Cloud Code, which if you've been watching me for a while, you probably are, you can stick around for the advanced tips later because I'll be showing how to best use Cloud Code and Grokbot together for you to achieve the outcomes that you need.

18
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:41,956
Number two is letting your Grokbot hire other bots.

19
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:46,880
Now, once you have some business context with your grok bot, you can now have it recommend new agents that you can create.

20
00:01:47,118 --> 00:01:50,720
Now, the great thing with Grokbot is that it is an orchestrator by design.

21
00:01:50,894 --> 00:01:56,800
And so you can just tell your main Grokbot, which for me you can see I named as Groko, to create those other agents for you.

22
00:01:57,006 --> 00:02:00,000
It now sets up the new bot with its own job, puts it in your sidebar.

23
00:02:00,334 --> 00:02:02,596
You can have it create multiple agents in one go.

24
00:02:02,830 --> 00:02:07,040
Now the general guideline is, especially for beginners, is that fewer agents is better.

25
00:02:07,278 --> 00:02:11,200
That's because by default, every one of these agents does keep its own memory.

26
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:17,040
So the more bots that you have, the more that you end up having to repeat yourself and the harder they are to manage as well.

27
00:02:17,358 --> 00:02:28,320
Now we'll go more into shared memory later in the video, but just by principle, it's similar to if you're a manager, you probably don't want to have a hundred people reporting to you because that will be such a headache in order for you to manage.

28
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:34,640
Instead, what you want is a core team of really strong team members whom you can train up to automate a lot of your business work.

29
00:02:34,894 --> 00:02:37,028
And this principle, by the way, is not entirely for myself.

30
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:41,124
This is coming from Lauren Tan, who's the principal engineer at SpaceX AI.

31
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:46,640
And even she says that a few good bots can be more useful and easier to manage than a large team.

32
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:48,400
Number three is Dr. Eggbot.

33
00:02:48,526 --> 00:02:56,164
Dr. Eggbot is from Lauren Tan herself, who, as I mentioned, is a principal engineer on Grokbot and has been sharing a lot of great tips on using it over at X.

34
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:01,280
Now she shared this bot template called Dr. Eggbot, which is basically a bot designer.

35
00:03:01,518 --> 00:03:04,004
So its only job is designing your other bots.

36
00:03:04,494 --> 00:03:15,012
So what you can do to use it is to import it from within their official marketplace with the link that you see on the screen, then just tell it the job that you want your next bot to do, and it builds that bot for you as per best practices.

37
00:03:15,310 --> 00:03:22,800
Mainly the principle behind this bot designer is that by default, it just asks a few questions, then it builds each new agent with one clear job.

38
00:03:22,958 --> 00:03:24,640
So it starts out with a goal already.

39
00:03:24,878 --> 00:03:31,440
Each bot also gets a short list of what Lauren is calling as anti-jobs, which are basically the things that that bot must never do.

40
00:03:31,726 --> 00:03:39,876
So if you don't know how to create great agents if you're a beginner, then just import this Dr. Eggbot template or even just send it to your main agent to adopt its principles.

41
00:03:40,174 --> 00:03:42,372
Next up is naming your bots well.

42
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:46,640
Because some people give their bots short generic names like Jason or Steve.

43
00:03:46,766 --> 00:03:51,520
And some people like to go really long, like content researcher or a scripting social media assistant.

44
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:58,084
Now, this is entirely based on your preference, and you can always change the names of your agents just by describing it to your main bot.

45
00:03:58,254 --> 00:04:03,392
But I actually think that the name matters more than most people believe, because it's how you find a bot in the sidebar.

46
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:08,260
So if it's like a common name like Jason, then you probably would need to understand what Jason even does.

47
00:04:08,526 --> 00:04:16,752
But if it's a long name, when you want to mention it in a chat, like you want your main Grok bot to assign a task to this agent, then you will need to mention that really long name every time.

48
00:04:17,102 --> 00:04:27,140
So to me, and how I set up my own space is probably somewhere in the middle where the sweet spot is a name that's short enough to type or even mention in a voice note, but it's clear enough that you know the job at a glance.

49
00:04:27,502 --> 00:04:35,232
So to give an example for my Grok bot workspace, I just have a simple naming convention where Groko is my main agent and then Seko is my security agent.

50
00:04:35,312 --> 00:04:38,564
I also have travel, which helps me a lot with a lot of our travel arrangements.

51
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:47,140
And so this naming convention works for me because they're names that's short enough to type in a chat and also clear enough that I know exactly what the job of this bot is at a glance.

52
00:04:47,392 --> 00:05:02,352
Next is around one of the most effective automations that you can set up with Grokbot in the very beginning, which is automating your inbox, where essentially you just have your agent connect to the emails that you want to automate and it can give you a digest of your emails for that day that can be as simple or as detailed as you want.

53
00:05:02,638 --> 00:05:07,872
How grokbot does this is through connectors, which are basically a direct line into applications like Gmail.

54
00:05:08,046 --> 00:05:13,792
To set it up, in case you want to click through things, you can open connect apps in the sidebar, then press connect next to Gmail.

55
00:05:13,934 --> 00:05:18,672
Or alternatively, you can just tell your Grockbot to connect to your emails and it will guide you on how to do it.

56
00:05:18,894 --> 00:05:26,512
And once you have your agent connected to the emails that you want, and yes, you can connect multiple ones, you can just ask it to give you a daily digest of your emails for that day.

57
00:05:26,734 --> 00:05:33,792
So what I do personally is I ask for one line per email and I just have it highlight the ones that it thinks matters a lot via an emoji.

58
00:05:33,872 --> 00:05:44,580
And also I just make it have a summary of the important ones up top so that when I read through this every morning, I see the most important ones up top, and I can just quickly eyeball what needs me without missing any of the emails that we received.

59
00:05:44,942 --> 00:05:50,592
And if you want the same format of an email daily digest as well, then this is a simple prompt that you can use to get started.

60
00:05:50,798 --> 00:06:01,072
And by the way, I put every one of these prompts, these tips, and all the resources I mentioned here into this very detailed PDF guide, which you can just send that to your Grokbot and ask it which ones fit your setup.

61
00:06:01,166 --> 00:06:03,712
The link for that is just in the description below if you need it.

62
00:06:04,014 --> 00:06:06,112
Next one related to email is cards.

63
00:06:06,510 --> 00:06:12,912
The great thing with Grokbot is that by default, your bot's emails and even Slack messages wait as cards for your review.

64
00:06:13,070 --> 00:06:17,028
And you can actually edit every email that it drafts for you before clicking send.

65
00:06:17,232 --> 00:06:22,592
That way, even when your agent has access to send emails for you, you still get to check what it wrote before it goes out.

66
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:30,272
Now you can obviously turn this off just by telling your agent to turn it off, but for beginners, you probably want to leave this on so that you can review what it writes every time.

67
00:06:30,478 --> 00:06:32,548
The next one is talking instead of typing.

68
00:06:32,752 --> 00:06:37,712
For most people, talking is much faster and you can transmit more information versus having to type.

69
00:06:38,126 --> 00:06:42,244
So in Grogbot, you can just hit the mic icon, braindump the whole job or task that you have.

70
00:06:42,638 --> 00:06:49,392
And a good tip that is also coming from Lauren is to have your agent repeat back to you what you just said and what its understanding of your intention is.

71
00:06:49,646 --> 00:06:54,512
And so that way you can just ramble to your bot and just keep talking to get a lot of information in.

72
00:06:54,702 --> 00:07:01,552
And when your agent restates it, it shows you straight away if it misunderstood anything before proceeding, especially for a big job.

73
00:07:01,774 --> 00:07:06,992
And by the way, if you're on desktop, control D or Command D is the hotkey or shortcut to do this as well.

74
00:07:07,246 --> 00:07:10,148
So you can just use that in order to dictate to your crock bot.

75
00:07:10,352 --> 00:07:12,196
Next one is voice memo replies.

76
00:07:12,590 --> 00:07:18,752
So we just talked about you talking to your bot, and this one flips it around because your bot can actually reply to you with a voice memo.

77
00:07:18,862 --> 00:07:24,032
It shows up in the chat like a voice note, and when you choose play, then you will now have that voice memo that you can listen to.

78
00:07:24,206 --> 00:07:29,312
You can also expand it to read the transcript, and clicking a word in that transcript jumps to the audio to that spot.

79
00:07:29,454 --> 00:07:33,552
Now, this is pretty useful when you're on the go and can't really read a long answer on your phone.

80
00:07:33,774 --> 00:07:39,300
So just know that that option is available and you can trigger that just by telling your agent to reply as a voice memo.

81
00:07:39,472 --> 00:07:45,472
Now, as an additional technique for a real back and forth, you can use voice mode, which is that waveform icon at the bottom right.

82
00:07:45,774 --> 00:07:50,432
So if you press that, it's basically like calling your agent where you can just speak to it and then it speaks back.

83
00:07:50,638 --> 00:07:52,112
The next one is around skills.

84
00:07:52,398 --> 00:07:57,952
So Grokbot has skills as well, which are basically save set of instructions that your bot can run on command.

85
00:07:58,222 --> 00:08:04,832
And basically the principle here is that if you end up typing the same instructions twice, it's probably better to turn that into a skill.

86
00:08:05,102 --> 00:08:12,112
So for example, right after your email digest from earlier, you can just say to save what we just did as a skill called slash email, for example.

87
00:08:12,494 --> 00:08:15,428
And next time you want an email digest, you can just type slash email.

88
00:08:15,630 --> 00:08:17,792
Your agent can also recommend skills for you too.

89
00:08:17,966 --> 00:08:28,112
And this is pretty simple if you use the prompt on screen that can get you started with your bot recommending to you which instructions it received from you multiple times, that is probably worth turning into a skill.

90
00:08:28,272 --> 00:08:30,192
Next is finding your usage hog.

91
00:08:30,702 --> 00:08:32,900
So you do have usage limits in Grokbot as well.

92
00:08:33,070 --> 00:08:36,676
And the more bots and skills that you use, the more that your usage drains.

93
00:08:37,102 --> 00:08:40,416
You can check how much you have left under settings, under usage and billing.

94
00:08:40,622 --> 00:08:45,376
And the great thing is with Grokbot, your agents actually have visibility of your usage limits as well.

95
00:08:45,678 --> 00:08:49,216
So you can just ask them which of your bots use the most usage this week.

96
00:08:49,358 --> 00:08:52,176
And also even to rank them and suggest how to cut back.

97
00:08:52,366 --> 00:09:00,896
With that really simple prompt, it comes back with an analysis of which of your agents are actually working, and you can quickly look which particular bot is actually draining your usage.

98
00:09:01,056 --> 00:09:02,820
Next is to ask for pages.

99
00:09:03,182 --> 00:09:08,336
With a lot of things that you can do with your agent, the chat box is actually not the best way to read them.

100
00:09:08,654 --> 00:09:10,496
So grok bot has this feature called pages.

101
00:09:10,734 --> 00:09:14,884
Now if you've used Cloud Code before, these are basically Grok's version of artifacts.

102
00:09:15,216 --> 00:09:22,336
It's essentially a page where it can visually show a report, a tracker, a checklist, or even a dashboard in a more virtual manner.

103
00:09:22,542 --> 00:09:29,124
And each of these pages can also be shared with a link, which is a functionality that you might be familiar with with things like Google Docs.

104
00:09:29,582 --> 00:09:35,588
And so let's say if you want to turn that usage breakdown that we just did into a dashboard page, you can just ask your Grok bot to do that.

105
00:09:35,854 --> 00:09:38,096
And that page will be accessible to you whenever you need it.

106
00:09:38,318 --> 00:09:45,764
You can even ask your bot to update this page, let's say every Monday morning, so that you always see what agent is using up the most tokens for the past week.

107
00:09:45,936 --> 00:09:47,716
Next are sections and pins.

108
00:09:48,046 --> 00:09:52,176
Now, once you have a few bots going, you can actually group them into sections in the sidebar.

109
00:09:52,334 --> 00:09:56,452
And this just helps for better organization in order for you to find your agents better.

110
00:09:56,718 --> 00:10:02,692
So you can have a section for your business, one for your personal life, and you can even pin the bots that you use the most right to the top.

111
00:10:02,958 --> 00:10:06,468
And you can do all of that just by simply right-clicking on each of your bots.

112
00:10:06,830 --> 00:10:09,028
Number 13 are custom icons for your bot.

113
00:10:09,166 --> 00:10:14,436
And similar to the last tip, this is just around agent organization and making the tool a bit more fun to use.

114
00:10:14,702 --> 00:10:18,816
Because if you click in your agents icon, you can actually change the shape and color of your bot.

115
00:10:19,022 --> 00:10:22,084
And for Autumn, the Grokbot team also added a pumpkin shape in here.

116
00:10:22,176 --> 00:10:26,016
But you can also generate entirely new icons from here or even upload your own.

117
00:10:26,286 --> 00:10:30,176
Now, a pro tip here is that you actually don't need to manually change these yourself.

118
00:10:30,382 --> 00:10:37,296
If you just talk to your main bot, you can just tell it that say all your business bots you want them to be red and all your personal bots you want them to be blue.

119
00:10:37,376 --> 00:10:39,536
And it will go ahead and do those changes for you.

120
00:10:39,694 --> 00:10:43,376
And so that way you can get more organized once you have multiple bots working for you.

121
00:10:43,456 --> 00:10:57,296
And if you want to get really deep into designing your own bots, this project called OpenBot Avatar, which is open source, you can just access it through this GitHub link, give that to your agent, and it can create grok bot style icons and animations for pretty much any shape and style that you want.

122
00:10:57,486 --> 00:11:11,936
And by the way, if you want to learn how to build and sell AI systems, that businesses actually pay for, then that's pretty much all we do over at the Robonuggets community, where not only do you get access to the Claude Living Masterclass, which we update every week and takes you from zero to mastery with the latest on AI.

123
00:11:12,016 --> 00:11:18,976
but you also get access to our agents as a service course, which walks you through how to actually get paid for all these AI skills that you are learning.

124
00:11:19,056 --> 00:11:22,496
You also get to be part of a genuinely great community of AI builders.

125
00:11:22,670 --> 00:11:26,628
In fact, you can see just some of the recent wins our members are getting from the program right here.

126
00:11:26,830 --> 00:11:29,936
So if you want to start earning from AI, then check that just in the pinned comment below.

127
00:11:30,030 --> 00:11:30,816
Now back to the video.

128
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:34,896
All right, so those easy tips already put you ahead of most people using Grogbot actually.

129
00:11:35,054 --> 00:11:41,616
But now for the intermediate tips, this is now where we take it to the next level where your bots can start working on their own even while you're asleep.

130
00:11:41,870 --> 00:11:51,936
The first thing to know is that your bot can now use the apps on your phone too, which means that it can actually create and schedule stuff for you, like adding events to your calendar, setting alarms even, and making reminders.

131
00:11:52,016 --> 00:11:54,496
So in a way, Grokbot is becoming like Siri.

132
00:11:54,736 --> 00:11:58,336
This was first announced on iPhone, but it now actually works on Android as well.

133
00:11:58,606 --> 00:12:02,564
So on both you get calendar, contacts, location, and even alarms.

134
00:12:02,798 --> 00:12:06,256
But do note that reminders and Apple Home are right now iPhone only.

135
00:12:06,574 --> 00:12:10,308
And to use this, just open settings in the grok bot app, then go to connect apps.

136
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:12,496
Choose your phone there, then press allow.

137
00:12:12,686 --> 00:12:18,256
Or similar to the other tips that I just mentioned, you can just ask your grok bot to do that and it will set it up for you automatically.

138
00:12:18,510 --> 00:12:20,656
Next is a one-press shortcut to your bot.

139
00:12:20,816 --> 00:12:26,416
If your iPhone has an action button, you can actually set it so that one-press starts a voice chat with your agent.

140
00:12:26,606 --> 00:12:29,764
And so this is what I mean when I said that Grokbot is becoming like Siri.

141
00:12:29,936 --> 00:12:37,856
Because if you suddenly thought of a task, then with just one-press, you can just hand that task to your agent the moment that you think of it without typing a single thing.

142
00:12:38,094 --> 00:12:41,136
To do it, just open your iPhone settings and choose the action button.

143
00:12:41,358 --> 00:12:46,052
Then if you swipe across the shortcut, you can choose the Grokbot app and pick Start Voice Chat.

144
00:12:46,176 --> 00:12:47,856
Next is the Chief of Staff technique.

145
00:12:48,110 --> 00:12:54,336
A chief of staff is basically the bot that checks what your other agents are waiting on you for and brings it back as one list.

146
00:12:54,574 --> 00:12:59,204
Now this works well because your bots can actually message each other and look into each other's conversations with you.

147
00:12:59,342 --> 00:13:01,696
So your main bot, for example, here I have Groco.

148
00:13:02,016 --> 00:13:13,696
Instead of me having to browse my multiple bots to see what they are waiting on, I just type slash /inbox and my main bot, looks at the conversations that I have across my workspace and tells me which one is waiting on me for a decision.

149
00:13:13,838 --> 00:13:23,696
Now this is pretty useful when you have some off time and you want to get some work done, or if you're finding that you have too many bots to manage and you actually don't want to individually go into each one's chat sessions every time.

150
00:13:23,950 --> 00:13:25,828
Number 17 is using routines.

151
00:13:26,222 --> 00:13:30,016
A routine is basically a job that your bot runs on its own on a schedule.

152
00:13:30,318 --> 00:13:33,156
And great thing about Grokbot is that automatically it runs in the cloud.

153
00:13:33,454 --> 00:13:36,736
So even with your phone or your laptop closed, it will still run.

154
00:13:37,038 --> 00:13:51,616
And so if you want to make that chief of staff technique into a routine, just tell your agent to do that so that every morning at 8 a.m., for example, have it tell you what your agents are waiting on you for, or even a summary of what your agents did during the past day, so that you can have that digest ready for you when you need it.

155
00:13:51,776 --> 00:13:54,756
Next and a related one is the concept of passive routines.

156
00:13:55,150 --> 00:14:00,736
Now, passive routines, unlike other scheduled tasks, stay quiet and they only message you when something needs you.

157
00:14:00,896 --> 00:14:07,536
And this is an important concept to know because the more agents and routines that you have, sometimes the more that you have to review.

158
00:14:07,616 --> 00:14:14,016
And that gets kind of stressful fast when every single runover routine sends you a message and is pining for your attention.

159
00:14:14,254 --> 00:14:22,096
And so passive or quiet routines are good because they run in the background and they only ping you whenever your agent deems it necessary to bug you with that information.

160
00:14:22,382 --> 00:14:25,636
For example, I have this bot called Psycho, which is my security bot.

161
00:14:25,966 --> 00:14:37,056
So with all the hacks and cybersecurity concerns that have been happening recently, it's actually a good idea to have a security bot that checks and alerts you for any leaks and hacks that you might need to be aware of, especially if it's relevant for your business.

162
00:14:37,230 --> 00:14:47,616
So what this does is it just scans the web every few hours for some of those stuff, but it only messages me when it deems that certain news topic to be important to our business or my work.

163
00:14:47,854 --> 00:14:52,324
And so this way I have some assurance that I get alerted as soon as possible if there's something that breaks.

164
00:14:52,654 --> 00:14:57,284
But because it's a quiet routine, then it doesn't disturb me so much with too many notifications.

165
00:14:57,678 --> 00:15:03,136
Next is creating skills by showing instead of telling, because your bot actually has its own computer.

166
00:15:03,438 --> 00:15:07,536
And if you're using the desktop app, you can actually open its computer view and watch it work.

167
00:15:07,696 --> 00:15:13,216
You can even take control of its computer in order to show your bot what you need to do for that particular website.

168
00:15:13,518 --> 00:15:17,536
So when you press take control here, you can drive this yourself from your own desktop.

169
00:15:17,710 --> 00:15:24,096
And so if you want to create a skill or a routine where you need to interface with a web page, this is probably something that you want to try out.

170
00:15:24,302 --> 00:15:33,696
Because instead of typing out or even describing what the bot needs to do in this page, it is often much easier to just show it and click through it yourself once and just have your agent remember it.

171
00:15:33,902 --> 00:15:40,736
And all you need to do to do it is to ask your agent to open its computer view because you're about to teach it a task that you want embedded into a skill.

172
00:15:40,910 --> 00:15:45,136
Number 20 is turning skills into deterministic code, at least as much as you can.

173
00:15:45,294 --> 00:15:50,016
And it is important because the more routines that you make, the more that your usage drains as well.

174
00:15:50,286 --> 00:16:01,376
But if you turn some of those routines into deterministic code, which basically means old-fashioned code that can run without an LLM, then you may actually find that some routines can be converted into that to save you some usage.

175
00:16:01,774 --> 00:16:09,856
Now, obviously, since this is going to be a script or code, it will need to be codified in a way where it does the exact same steps every time without any AI thinking needed.

176
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:14,816
But the good thing about it is that your agent can probably audit your routines and see where the opportunities are.

177
00:16:14,990 --> 00:16:16,804
And you can get started with a prompt like this.

178
00:16:17,038 --> 00:16:21,616
Next up is learning to let your bots communicate because your growth bots can actually pass work to each other.

179
00:16:21,966 --> 00:16:23,940
And it helps to look under the hood to see how this works.

180
00:16:24,206 --> 00:16:33,872
Because a lot of people assume that all of their bots share one memory, which makes sense because they run on the same computer technically, but each of these bots actually is keeping its own memory in chats.

181
00:16:34,030 --> 00:16:37,508
So think of them as like separate file folders in that computer that they're running on.

182
00:16:37,806 --> 00:16:42,820
So your finance bot knows your invoices and cash flows, but not necessarily your marketing campaigns.

183
00:16:43,182 --> 00:16:50,992
By default, only a few facts like your name and your time zone and whichever your bots deem necessary to share are the only ones shared across all of them.

184
00:16:51,214 --> 00:16:53,472
And so there's two ways by which you can let your bots communicate.

185
00:16:53,646 --> 00:16:57,312
The first one is handoffs, where you just have your bot message each other directly.

186
00:16:57,486 --> 00:17:02,592
And you can do this in plain words like have the finance agent ask the marketing bot which campaigns ran last month.

187
00:17:02,752 --> 00:17:05,476
Or another feature that you can use is the bot group chats.

188
00:17:05,806 --> 00:17:14,112
So if you ask the finance agent to create a group chat with the marketing agent, then it will create a new thread in your sidebar where those two agents can just share relevant information to each other.

189
00:17:14,272 --> 00:17:16,032
You can put multiple bots in just one chat.

190
00:17:16,174 --> 00:17:18,432
and this works pretty well for more project-based work.

191
00:17:18,702 --> 00:17:22,592
So try it out if in case you think you need multiple agents to work on a single project.

192
00:17:22,894 --> 00:17:33,540
And by the way, you can also start these group chats by creating a new chat, Command Shift N on a Mac or Control Shift N on Windows, then just add both bots in the To field, similar to how you would do it in other messaging apps.

193
00:17:33,838 --> 00:17:36,388
Next up is knowing the concept of their shared brains.

194
00:17:36,750 --> 00:17:41,152
Now in a group chat, the bots can read each other's replies, but they still don't share the same memory.

195
00:17:41,312 --> 00:17:43,632
And that is where the shared brain concept comes in.

196
00:17:43,790 --> 00:17:48,964
Because in the computer that your Grok bots are using, you can basically imagine it as one folder that every bot can read.

197
00:17:49,166 --> 00:17:57,764
And this is useful if you want to implement rules that you want to stay through across all bots, or if you want your agents to adhere to a certain design system when creating pages or artifacts.

198
00:17:58,158 --> 00:18:03,300
Now, by default, GrokBot decides what to put into the shared brain, but you can actually be more intentional about this.

199
00:18:03,470 --> 00:18:13,700
So if you ask your main bot to move what all your bots should know into a shared brain folder, then it will recommend some of those information pieces and artifacts that would be useful for every agent to have access to.

200
00:18:13,872 --> 00:18:15,392
Next are event triggers.

201
00:18:15,726 --> 00:18:23,780
Now you might think a routine only runs on a schedule, like for example, my security bot from earlier, but it can also actually wake up and activate the moment something happens.

202
00:18:24,078 --> 00:18:25,860
So that is what we call event triggers.

203
00:18:26,062 --> 00:18:28,512
And they are called that because they are triggered based on an event.

204
00:18:28,592 --> 00:18:33,124
Like for example, if they receive a Slack message or an email to its own address.

205
00:18:33,422 --> 00:18:39,312
If you're more advanced, there are also triggers for work tools like GitHub, and you can also implement webhooks that direct your agent.

206
00:18:39,406 --> 00:18:47,552
But the difference of this versus normal routines is once that event goes to your GrocBot, they get started right away instead of being activated on a certain time schedule.

207
00:18:47,758 --> 00:18:56,272
To give one example, you can set up a form on your website that uses a webhook underneath it that basically sends the information that is filled up in that form to your agent.

208
00:18:56,398 --> 00:19:03,232
And once that agent receives it, it then acts on it, like it drafts a reply, it can provide the resource that you promised on that website, and so on and so forth.

209
00:19:03,406 --> 00:19:06,352
So to set this up, all you need is a prompt like this to get you started.

210
00:19:06,542 --> 00:19:13,284
Next is getting your agent its own inbox because your grokbot can actually claim its own email address at mail.grokbot.com.

211
00:19:13,472 --> 00:19:16,752
And to do this, you just need to ask your main bot to get this email address for you.

212
00:19:17,038 --> 00:19:20,352
Just to note though that as of right now, you only get one address per account.

213
00:19:20,494 --> 00:19:24,900
So across all of your grokbot agents, they will share just one email address in this domain.

214
00:19:25,102 --> 00:19:28,752
And you also can't rename or delete it as of the moment, so you need to pick the name well.

215
00:19:28,912 --> 00:19:42,672
It's also good to know this now and to claim this now because similar to com websites, for example, if you want your business name to own this handle, especially as Grokbot gains more popularity, then it's probably good to reserve this email in this domain already while it is early.

216
00:19:42,958 --> 00:19:48,324
And so with this email address, it can also sign up to things like newsletters and even create accounts in the websites that you use.

217
00:19:48,622 --> 00:19:50,660
The next technique is called routine scout.

218
00:19:50,798 --> 00:19:54,596
Now, a routine scout is basically a routine that suggests other routines.

219
00:19:54,926 --> 00:20:01,348
For example, once a week, it can look at what you've been asking your bots to do and it can give you a short rank list of ideas in its chat.

220
00:20:01,518 --> 00:20:04,900
Because the reality is most of us only automate the jobs that we think of.

221
00:20:05,102 --> 00:20:16,836
But if your bot can spot the ones that you keep asking for by hand, which works because they have your chat history anyway, then it can offer to take work off of your plate now by suggesting routines that automatically run that parts of your business.

222
00:20:17,230 --> 00:20:29,152
And to do this, you can just use a prompt like this to get started, send that to your main bot, and even adjust the frequency and the number of new routines that you want suggested to you, so that over time you can make your Grokbots more automated the more that you use this tool.

223
00:20:29,358 --> 00:20:36,672
An add-on tip related to this is that Grokbots, remember, can actually search X on their own because Grokbot and X are owned by the same company.

224
00:20:36,846 --> 00:20:40,432
And so I'm finding that a lot of really good tips are actually coming from X itself.

225
00:20:40,750 --> 00:20:54,592
And so if you ask your Grokbot to also scan X for any tips that you can use for Grokbot, then it will pull techniques and tips that it will find on X, including the most recent ones that may be relevant to you and your business, and include that in the automations that it will suggest for you.

226
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:56,260
All right, now for the advanced tips.

227
00:20:56,686 --> 00:21:00,672
And for this section, Lauren from the Grokbot team actually has a great way to think about this part.

228
00:21:01,070 --> 00:21:05,616
Because for you to be effective and really get the most out of your grok bots, you need to think like a manager.

229
00:21:05,934 --> 00:21:11,428
So the question now becomes how do you get your bots to do your work as well as you, or even better, so you're free to do bigger things.

230
00:21:11,598 --> 00:21:20,656
And remember, if any of these feel a bit too technical, just hand that PDF guide to your bot that you can just get down below and ask it to either explain these concepts for you or even set them up.

231
00:21:20,816 --> 00:21:22,148
First up is Vault Payments.

232
00:21:22,510 --> 00:21:33,216
Now the Vault in Grokbot is basically where your agents keep your cards and logins and it fills them in at checkout without actually seeing the numbers, the same way that Apple Pay or Google Pay is working now under the hood.

233
00:21:33,390 --> 00:21:38,084
It lives in settings and under payments is where you can pick the card that your bots are going to use first.

234
00:21:38,318 --> 00:21:44,016
Each card can also have a hint, by the way, which is a short note that tells your agents when and where to use it.

235
00:21:44,238 --> 00:21:52,576
Now, apart from card details, the vault can also store personal logins and other private information that you want your bots to be able to use, but just have it in a more secure location.

236
00:21:52,814 --> 00:21:55,748
Next is that you can now use your agents to buy from within the chat.

237
00:21:55,950 --> 00:22:02,440
Because when you ask for something that you want to buy, your bot now shows product cards with photos, a rating, the price, and the store.

238
00:22:02,670 --> 00:22:05,668
So it's a similar experience to what you would expect in a site like Amazon.

239
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:12,996
You even have a buy now button straight from the agent, where after that your bot basically tracks the order for you and tells you when it ships or arrives.

240
00:22:13,326 --> 00:22:14,976
Next is setting up one password.

241
00:22:15,214 --> 00:22:21,296
So if you use one password, which I highly recommend, this app is basically a tool that keeps all of your passwords in one place.

242
00:22:21,678 --> 00:22:23,684
And Grokbot now has direct integration to it.

243
00:22:23,950 --> 00:22:30,496
So your logins can stay in your own one password vault, which just provides you better assurance that your agents will never see your password.

244
00:22:30,734 --> 00:22:37,056
And to set this up, all you do is open Connect Apps, the same place we connected Gmail earlier, then search for OnePassword and click add.

245
00:22:37,390 --> 00:22:40,096
Next is that Grokbot can now make phone calls for you.

246
00:22:40,206 --> 00:22:43,076
Because a lot of today's business still happens via phone calls.

247
00:22:43,342 --> 00:22:48,324
And it is quite useful if in case you need to follow an order or inquire about details of a particular business.

248
00:22:48,496 --> 00:22:53,456
Now do note that as of the time of this recording, it is only available in the US at the moment.

249
00:22:53,742 --> 00:22:57,216
So they're probably rolling this out in steps because of how powerful it can be.

250
00:22:57,422 --> 00:23:04,256
And to be honest, once this is fully rolled out, they probably will set limits or else we'll have multiple agents just calling multiple people all the time.

251
00:23:04,496 --> 00:23:08,176
But for now, if you're in the US, then you can try this out yourself to see if it works for you.

252
00:23:08,336 --> 00:23:10,736
Next is making sure not to skip alignment.

253
00:23:10,862 --> 00:23:16,516
If you do actual end-to-end projects with your bot, you need to learn to align with your bots before doing the big task.

254
00:23:16,750 --> 00:23:21,540
And basically, this just means that your bot asks you questions before that big job starts.

255
00:23:21,902 --> 00:23:31,136
This is useful because if you send just a generic prompt for a big ask, the final output that they will give you is possibly off spec and they would end up just wasting your usage.

256
00:23:31,438 --> 00:23:34,496
What I do personally is I use this skill called slash align.

257
00:23:34,638 --> 00:23:38,256
And I know another popular one is called Grill Me, which is pretty much the same thing.

258
00:23:38,414 --> 00:23:49,216
But basically for my skill, that just gives me numbered questions as well as recommendations on each one so that I can just quickly respond and choose to make sure that we are aligned before it goes and fires off the big task.

259
00:23:49,422 --> 00:23:51,856
Next is letting your Grokbot use your own computer.

260
00:23:52,078 --> 00:23:57,956
Now, by default, your bot does work on its own computer in the cloud, the one that keeps running even with your laptop closed.

261
00:23:58,190 --> 00:24:05,536
But you can also actually let it work on your own computer with all of the files that you have there, as long as your device is open and GrokBot is running on it.

262
00:24:05,678 --> 00:24:11,136
And this is useful if you have spreadsheets, photos, and basically any file that just lives on your computer right now.

263
00:24:11,374 --> 00:24:16,016
That instead of you having to upload and send it to Grokbot, you can just have it fetch those things for you.

264
00:24:16,206 --> 00:24:27,940
This is also useful if you are using Cloud Code, because what you can do is just have Grokbot look into your Cloud skills and identify the ones that it can import in its memory so that you can use it from within Grokbot itself.

265
00:24:28,206 --> 00:24:34,176
To do this, just open settings, then computer, and you can choose in here how much level of access you will give to your grokbot by default.

266
00:24:34,350 --> 00:24:37,828
Next is setting up a cloud code or codex bridge, depending on what you use.

267
00:24:38,126 --> 00:24:43,616
Because if you use Cloud Code, for example, it can actually hand your Grokbot jobs through a webhook routine.

268
00:24:43,854 --> 00:24:53,296
And so if you need Cloud to hand off tasks to Grokbot or even export your skills or pieces of your business memory and context from Cloud Code into Grokbot, then this is a good solution to do.

269
00:24:53,456 --> 00:25:00,004
This also effectively lets Cloud Code use what your bot is good at, such as, for example, searching X with no setup or extra fees.

270
00:25:00,336 --> 00:25:03,776
To get started, you can just use the prompt on the screen and send that to Cloud Code.

271
00:25:03,886 --> 00:25:08,176
Now, a more advanced concept of this to take it to the next level is the concept of a central computer.

272
00:25:08,430 --> 00:25:16,416
Because with the Cloud Code and Grokbot setup we said earlier, that basically has to run on a device that needs to stay on for Cloud Code to even run in the first place.

273
00:25:16,814 --> 00:25:23,680
Now, a central computer is basically one always-on machine where all of your files, your skills, and even your business context and memory live.

274
00:25:23,854 --> 00:25:27,040
And you just have Cloud Code and GrokBot both connect to it.

275
00:25:27,246 --> 00:25:32,080
Now, to set this up, you first of course need the machine, which is going to be the central computer in this whole ecosystem.

276
00:25:32,270 --> 00:25:36,720
You can use a Mac mini or a mini PC with both Grokbot and Cloud Code installed.

277
00:25:36,910 --> 00:25:42,160
You can also use a VPS for this, which is a virtual private server, essentially a computer that you rent in the cloud.

278
00:25:42,350 --> 00:25:44,560
But I would recommend that generally if you know what you're doing.

279
00:25:44,718 --> 00:25:52,804
Because obviously, if you have a lot of sensitive files, having them placed in the VPS in the cloud increases the risk exposure of those files depending on the VPS provider that you use.

280
00:25:53,120 --> 00:25:57,280
Now, this is a bigger topic, so just comment down below if you want a whole lesson on it.

281
00:25:57,518 --> 00:25:58,880
Next is keeping a backlog.

282
00:25:59,246 --> 00:26:03,120
So a backlog is basically one list of every task that you want to get to at some point.

283
00:26:03,374 --> 00:26:12,320
In a lot of tools, you can sort these tasks by priority, and you can also have a Kanban board, which clearly shows to you which one is to do, which one is in progress, and which tasks are done.

284
00:26:12,526 --> 00:26:16,720
Now, this is useful because in your day-to-day work, you probably have tasks that you want to set aside for the next time.

285
00:26:16,814 --> 00:26:20,260
And the chat interface is simply not the best UX for that purpose.

286
00:26:20,590 --> 00:26:23,600
And so just see in your day-to-day work if you now need a backlog.

287
00:26:24,014 --> 00:26:28,640
Now there's multiple apps that do this out of the box, like Linear, for example, which you can connect to your Grokbot.

288
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:35,040
Todoist is also a simpler app that you can use, or you can even create a Grokbot page that functions as your backlog.

289
00:26:35,342 --> 00:26:38,000
And you can get started with that with a prompt like this that you see on the screen.

290
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:40,160
Next is the concept of a bot report card.

291
00:26:40,430 --> 00:26:47,332
A bot report card is basically a weekly check where one bot scores your other bots out of 10 and suggests how to fix the weak ones.

292
00:26:47,598 --> 00:26:56,160
You can give this task to Dr. Eggbot from earlier, and this is useful because once you have a few bots, it actually gets hard to tell which one keeps slipping and which one is draining your usage.

293
00:26:56,334 --> 00:27:01,220
So again, this is part of the concept of thinking like a manager and seeing how you can best manage your agents.

294
00:27:01,518 --> 00:27:03,940
And so this is sort of like an HR for your other bots.

295
00:27:04,110 --> 00:27:10,148
And to do it, all you do is open that bot that you want to assign as HR and ask for a simple routine with a prompt like this.

296
00:27:10,510 --> 00:27:12,400
Next is sharing bots as a template.

297
00:27:12,526 --> 00:27:19,012
Now you can turn any bot into a template, actually, which is basically a version of that bot that other people can add from simply a link.

298
00:27:19,310 --> 00:27:25,156
What travels with it though is just the bot's name, description, skills, and routines, but never your computer, your logins, or your sensitive files.

299
00:27:25,454 --> 00:27:31,268
To do this, just click the bot's name at the top of the chat and choose share and choose create a copy, which gives you that link.

300
00:27:31,630 --> 00:27:33,280
And lastly is the bot marketplace.

301
00:27:33,550 --> 00:27:40,356
So if templates are how you share your own bots, the bot marketplace, the official one on x.ai, is the place for bots that other people build.

302
00:27:40,494 --> 00:27:44,580
It has categories like sales and marketing and a section from the grokbot team themselves.

303
00:27:44,974 --> 00:27:52,740
Now, a pro tip is to send this link to your main agent and just ask that main agent to recommend any templates in there that would be applicable to your business.

304
00:27:53,166 --> 00:27:53,600
So there you go.

305
00:27:53,680 --> 00:27:56,080
That is all tips and techniques for Grokbot.

306
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:57,680
And as usual, thanks for watching till the end.

307
00:27:57,760 --> 00:28:02,160
I usually do videos like these where the intention is to help you use these agents better for your business.

308
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:04,640
So if that's your thing, then feel free to subscribe down below.

309
00:28:04,846 --> 00:28:05,600
I'll see you next time.

310
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:06,400
Cheers

